This library is presented to the guest as unavailable: the runtime does not
open it, and binds nothing to it (a map target of "" in a build for one
application, Guest/absent.json in a generic build).

This is a workaround, not how macOS behaves: the game it was written for links
MetalFX with LC_LOAD_DYLIB (not weak), so dyld on a Mac without MetalFX would
refuse to launch it. It imports no MetalFX symbol and reaches MetalFX only
through Objective-C runtime lookups, which find nothing while the real
framework is not loaded; the game then takes its non-MetalFX path. With
MetalFX loaded it crashed in an early initializer on iPadOS (root cause not
known); MetalFX is absent from the iOS simulator, where the same startup
succeeds. classify.py warns if an executable has strong imports from it.
